\paragraph{Effect of sample count.}
Increasing $N$ from 2 to 32 expands oracle headroom sixfold ($+3.3$ to ${\sim}{+}20$~pp) but yields no commensurate selection improvement: LP selection improves monotonically with $N$ ($+0.42$~pp at $N{=}8$ to $+0.91$~pp at $N{=}32$), but even at $N{=}32$---where degeneracy falls to 4\% and oracle headroom exceeds 20~pp---the best signal captures less than 5\% of the available improvement (Table~\ref{tab:n_scaling_selection}).
VC selection degrades with increasing $N$ ($-2.27$~pp at $N{=}2$ to $-4.18$~pp at $N{=}32$), consistent with mode-seeking bias in voting-based selection as the candidate pool grows.

\footnotetext{$N$-scaling analysis uses subsampled evaluation on the full 551-instance test set. Absolute greedy F1 (0.614) differs from Table~\ref{tab:selection} (0.645) due to the subsampling procedure and instance weighting; relative trends are consistent.}

\paragraph{Bootstrap significance at $N{=}32$.}
Table~\ref{tab:bootstrap_n32} reports bootstrap confidence intervals and $p$-values for selection at $N{=}32$ ($B{=}10{,}000$, $n{=}529$).
No signal achieves significant improvement over greedy at $\alpha{=}0.05$ (LP: $p{=}0.091$; FK: $p{=}0.101$), while VC is significantly \textit{worse} ($-4.36$~pp, $p{=}1.0$).
However, all signals except VC significantly outperform random selection ($p{<}0.001$, permutation test), confirming that the signals carry meaningful quality information yet fail to translate it into reliable selection gains.
The oracle gap ($+20.0$~pp, $p{<}0.001$) confirms substantial unrealized headroom.

